NESTED INPUT TEST

Run tools/nested-input from a terminal in Hyprland. It opens the development
build with a foot terminal, prints the source commit and binary SHA-256,
and passes host shortcuts through only while that nested window has focus.
It forces the Wayland backend; it cannot become a hardware compositor.

Ctrl+Alt+Escape restores host shortcuts immediately and latches passthrough
off until the helper is undone and started again. Moving focus away also
restores host shortcuts; returning normally restores passthrough.

Run tools/nested-input --undo to remove its temporary binding and callbacks.
The wrapper also does this when its child exits or it receives a normal
termination signal. SIGKILL cannot run cleanup: use --undo afterward.
Undo does not terminate the nested compositor. Close it normally, or use
Meta+Shift+E while passthrough is active.

No persistent config is written and no reload is needed. Repeating undo is
safe. A second launcher refuses to replace an existing helper. The private
submap has no ordinary host bindings; universal bindings from other user
configuration can still apply. This is a Hyprland 0.56 Lua development aid,
not a tam-pane public API. Retire it when nested input testing no longer
needs host shortcut passthrough.

Acceptance: open two terminals with Meta+Return, cycle with Meta+J/K,
close with Meta+W, hold a typing key to check repeat, and move/click between
windows. Check Ctrl+Alt+Escape and host shortcuts after leaving passthrough.
Record Fred's results against the printed source and binary hash.

Checks: python3 -B tests/test_nested_input.py; make test.
